I made no such promise, but I still shouldn't.  I'd like to
point out that what XML does in a very bulky way with angle
braces and repeated tags for beginnings and endings, fully
parenthesized prefix notation has been doing relatively
concisely for decades with parentheses.  And LISP readers
and encoders are just as mature and featureful as XML readers
and encoders.

To those of us who use LISP, the success of XML is a good
reason for a belly laugh, especially since the same people
who love it so claimed to hate the simpler parentheses.

Of course, it's also cause for joy, since we've had some
idea of the power of S-expressions and interoperable readers
for decades and the rest of the world starting to catch up
means there's more stuff to operate on.

However, although XML (or Lisp notation) will help, it is
not going to completely solve the incompatible bones files
problem.  The problem is that in a lot of cases, the
incompatibilities arise because there's a *semantic* mismatch
between versions of the game.  An item that doesn't exist
in version B is in a bones file generated by version A.  An
item that behaves differently in version B is in a bones
file generated by version A.  And so on.

